# labwc autostart for the Home Assistant kiosk.
#
# On this Raspberry Pi OS image the session runs /usr/bin/labwc-pi, and BOTH
# /etc/xdg/labwc/autostart and this file are executed. (Generic labwc
# documentation says the user file replaces the system one; that is not what
# happens here. Verified by putting kanshi in both and finding two of it
# running.) So do not repeat the system file's entries - kanshi, pcmanfm-pi and
# wf-panel-pi all start from there already.
#
# The file manager and the panel therefore keep running, hidden behind the
# full-screen browser window. They cost a few tens of megabytes and nothing on
# screen, which is a fair trade for not fighting the system session.
#
# Every line needs a trailing &. Without one labwc blocks until that program
# exits and no later line ever runs.
#
# lwrespawn restarts the browser if it dies.

/usr/bin/lwrespawn "$HOME/.local/bin/ha-kiosk-launch" &
